<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Builds the digest and SignedInfo signing string for an enveloped XML
    signature — canonicalization, digest, and SignedInfo construction, all
    in Java — replacing the former JS-Build-XmlDsig-Signing-String
    (xmldsig-build-signing-string.js + the hand-rolled xml-exc-c14n.js
    canonicalizer). See java-src/README.md for build/deploy steps, and
    the class-level comment in XmlDsigSignCallout.java (and XmlC14n.java)
    for why this doesn't reuse javax.xml.crypto.dsig (JSR 105) the way
    the validator side's JC-XmlDsig-Verify-Signature does — generation
    needs the raw canonicalized SignedInfo bytes as an output (the actual
    RSA/ECDSA signing happens on an external API, not locally), which
    JSR 105's public API has no supported way to produce standalone.

    document/document-fallback mirror the validator side's own
    signature.verify.payload / message.content fallback: this signs
    signature.payload if the calling proxy staged one, else message.content
    — and immediately writes WHICHEVER one was actually used back into
    signature.payload, so JC-Assemble-XmlDsig-Signature (which resolves
    its own "document" Property the same way, but runs later, after the
    external signing-API call in between) is guaranteed to embed the
    exact same bytes that got hashed here, not whatever message.content
    happens to hold by the time it runs. See XmlDsigSignCallout.java's
    class comment for the real failure this closes: a calling proxy that
    never explicitly stages signature.payload (relying only on the
    message.content fallback) can otherwise end up with these two steps
    silently disagreeing if anything mutates message.content in between.

    signature-method is read from signature.xmldsig.signatureMethod if the
    calling proxy already set it; default-signature-method supplies
    "...xmldsig-more#rsa-sha256" otherwise — same auto-default behavior
    the JS version had, now literal config instead of an `if` in the
    script.

    Sets signature.xmldsig.signatureMethod (echoing back whichever value
    was actually used), signature.xmldsig.digest.value,
    signature.xmldsig.signedinfo.canonical, and
    signature.xmldsig.signing.string — the exact same flow variables the
    JS step set, so NI-Signature-XmlDsig-Sign (still a placeholder for the
    external signing-API call and final <ds:Signature> assembly) needs no
    changes. continueOnError is "true" so a malformed document fails via
    signature.xmldsig.error rather than a generic Apigee JavaCallout
    fault — there's no RaiseFault step checking it yet since the JS
    version this replaces didn't have one either (it just threw, which
    Apigee turns into a fault); add one alongside
    NI-Signature-XmlDsig-Sign if you want this guarded the same way the
    validator side guards its own steps.
-->
<JavaCallout name="JC-Build-XmlDsig-Signing-String" continueOnError="true">
    <DisplayName>JC-Build-XmlDsig-Signing-String</DisplayName>
    <ClassName>com.orders.signature.XmlDsigSignCallout</ClassName>
    <ResourceURL>java://xmldsig-sign-callout.jar</ResourceURL>
    <Properties>
        <Property name="document">{signature.payload}</Property>
        <Property name="document-fallback">{message.content}</Property>
        <Property name="signature-method">{signature.xmldsig.signatureMethod}</Property>
        <Property name="default-signature-method">http://www.w3.org/2001/04/xmldsig-more#rsa-sha256</Property>
        <Property name="output-prefix">signature.xmldsig</Property>
    </Properties>
</JavaCallout>
